[FINAL-TEXT] I now have a complete picture of the topology. All four web servers (10.200.0.3–10.200.0.6) are up, Apache is listening on port 80, and all serve correct content. DNS resolves web0.local → 10.200.0.3 and web1.local → 10.200.0.4. End-to-end `curl http://web0.local/` from host_1_1_1_1 returns HTTP 200. The team's hypothesis is completely wrong.

The sole confirmed anomaly is on **dns_server**: `named.service` is in a persistent `failed` state — the BIND daemon is running only because the Kathara startup script started it directly (`named -g -c /etc/bind/named.conf`, as root, outside systemd), so when systemd subsequently tried to start `named.service` it found port 53 already occupied and gave up after exhausting its restart attempts (`Restart=on-failure` is now inert for a unit in the `failed` state).

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dns_server
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is the BIND DNS daemon (`named`) on `dns_server` (10.200.0.2). The systemd-managed unit `named.service` is in a persistent `failed` state — it has never successfully started under systemd control. The mechanism is a startup-ordering conflict: the Kathara container startup script launched named directly in foreground mode (`named -g -c /etc/bind/named.conf`) before systemd attempted to start `named.service`. When systemd ran `/usr/sbin/named -f -u bind`, port 53 was already occupied, named exited immediately, and after exhausting its `Restart=on-failure` retries systemd marked the unit as `failed` and stopped managing it entirely.

Because the manually-started process (pid 133) is still alive, DNS is currently functional — `dig @10.200.0.2 web0.local` returns 10.200.0.3 and `curl http://web0.local/` from host_1_1_1_1 returns HTTP 200. However, named is completely unsupervised: if it crashes there is no automatic recovery, and `systemctl restart named` cannot be used reliably while the rogue instance holds port 53.

All four web servers are healthy — Apache is listening on port 80, serving correct content, reachable by both IP and hostname from host_1_1_1_1. The team's belief that the web farm crashed is a false premise; rebooting them would fix nothing.

Root cause: `named.service` on `dns_server` failed to start under systemd because the container startup script pre-empted it by binding port 53 first, leaving the daemon unmanaged and `named.service` permanently `failed`.

Corrective fix: On `dns_server`, kill the orphaned named process (`pkill named`) and then run `systemctl restart named` to bring the daemon back under proper systemd supervision with automatic restart on failure.
=== END ===